---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import BlogPostHeader from '../../components/BlogPostHeader.astro';
import AppealLetter from '../../components/AppealLetter.astro';

const title = "What to Do if a Parking Company Asks for Your Driving Licence | AppealAirportPCN";
const description = "After an airport PCN appeal, the operator may ask for a copy of your driving licence. Here is a real example, what the rules say, and a short reply.";
const canonical = "https://appealairportpcn.co.uk/guides/driving-licence-request/";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  "headline": "What to do if a parking company asks for your driving licence",
  "description": description,
  "image": "https://appealairportpcn.co.uk/images/times24-photo-id-request.png",
  "mainEntityOfPage": canonical,
  "author": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "AppealAirportPCN" },
  "publisher": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "AppealAirportPCN.co.uk" },
  "datePublished": "2026-11-15",
  "dateModified": "2026-11-15"
};
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} schema={schema}>
  <BlogPostHeader
    title="What to do if a parking company asks for your driving licence"
    breadcrumbLabel="Driving licence requests"
    breadcrumbHref="/guides/driving-licence-request/"
    updatedDate="NOVEMBER 2026"
    fixedUpdatedDate={true}
  />
  <main>
    <div class="wrap">
      <article class="blog-post">
        <p>You appealed an airport drop-off PCN. The reply is not a decision. It asks for a copy of your driving licence.</p>
        <p>Our example comes from Gatwick, where the operator is Times24 UK Limited. Other operators may send similar requests.</p>
        <p>This guide shows the email, explains what it means, and gives a short reply you can send.</p>

        <h2>The email</h2>
        <figure class="protected-image">
          <img
            src="/images/times24-photo-id-request.png"
            alt="Redacted example of a Times24 UK Limited email requesting the photo ID section of a driving licence for a Gatwick Airport North Terminal appeal."
            width="984"
            height="768"
            loading="lazy"
            draggable="false"
          />
          <figcaption>Redacted example. Name, PCN number and date removed.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>The email says three things:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>It wants a copy of the photo ID section of your driving licence.</li>
          <li>It says this is to "verify the driver against unredacted images".</li>
          <li>It puts the notice on hold for 14 days while you reply. You can use their portal or send an email.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Your letter may differ. Check the deadline in yours.</p>

        <h2>What it means</h2>
        <p>The operator wants to know who was driving. If you send your licence, you confirm that you were the driver. It can then pursue you as the driver.</p>
        <p>You do not have to reply. But the hold is short. After it ends, the appeal will probably be decided on what they already have.</p>

        <h2>What the rules say</h2>
        <p>The Private Parking Sector Single Code of Practice (version 1.1) does not ban an operator from asking for ID. It also does not stop an operator making an appeal depend on naming the driver. So the request is not against the Code.</p>
        <p>The Code says the driver is liable for a parking charge (Annex C). A keeper who will not name the driver "may be assumed" to be the driver. A note in section 8 says this may be used as evidence. These are notes. They are best practice, not mandatory rules.</p>
        <p>Keeper liability comes from Schedule 4 of the Protection of Freedoms Act 2012 (POFA). It does not apply to land under statutory control. Our <a href="/guides/relevant-land-airport-pcns/">guide to relevant land</a> explains this.</p>
        <p>The Code also says charges issued under byelaws "may be different". It says operators must not confuse byelaw enforcement with contractual charges (3.1.3, Note 2).</p>

        <h2>What this means in practice</h2>
        <p>POFA does not let them pass the charge to you as keeper. But they can still argue that you were the driver. That is why they ask for your licence.</p>

        <h2>Your options</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>Send a short reply like the one below. You do not name the driver and you do not send ID.</li>
          <li>Do not reply. The notice will probably be decided on the existing evidence.</li>
          <li>If you were the driver, you may choose to say so. Do not say anything untrue.</li>
        </ol>

        <h2>A short reply you can send</h2>
        <AppealLetter airport="Gatwick Airport" letterId="driving-licence-reply">
          <p>I am appealing this Parking Charge Notice.</p>
          <p>I will not identify the driver or provide the driver's details. I am not obliged to provide a driving licence. The DVLA and BPA/IPC have told all members that appeals MUST NOT be or appear conditional on disclosing who was driving and further to this it will be reported to both DVLA and the IPC as breach of conduct.</p>
          <p>The alleged contravention took place on Gatwick Airport land. Gatwick's current published terms confirm that its airport land, buildings and roads are subject to its airport byelaws and other statutory controls.</p>
          <p>You cannot rely on Elliott v Loake (1982) to presume that the registered keeper was the driver. That criminal case turned on specific forensic evidence and proven falsehoods rather than creating a general civil presumption, and keeper liability in private parking requires strict compliance with Schedule 4 of the Protection of Freedoms Act 2012.</p>
          <p>Schedule 4 of the Protection of Freedoms Act 2012 excludes land where parking is subject to statutory control from the definition of relevant land. Keeper liability under Schedule 4 therefore does not arise in this case.</p>
          <p>I note that the current Private Parking Sector Single Code of Practice states that the driver is liable for a parking charge and that POFA provides the mechanism for transferring liability to a keeper where its conditions are met. The Code also expressly recognises that charges issued under byelaws may be different.</p>
          <p>As POFA keeper liability does not apply to this land, my refusal to identify the driver does not create keeper liability under Schedule 4.</p>
          <p>Please cancel the Parking Charge Notice.</p>
        </AppealLetter>
        <p>Send it by the portal or email address in the letter. Keep a copy and note the date.</p>

        <h2>What happens next</h2>
        <p>The operator will send a decision. It may reject the appeal and rely on Elliott v Loake. Our <a href="/guides/elliott-v-loake-why-it-doesnt-make-you-liable-as-the-keeper/">guide to Elliott v Loake</a> covers it.</p>
        <p>The rejection letter names the appeals service. POPLA is used for British Parking Association members. The IAS is used for International Parking Community members. Times24 at Gatwick named the IAS. In both cases, you have 28 days from the rejection to appeal.</p>

        <div class="callout">
          <strong>Key point:</strong> The operator can ask for your licence. You do not have to send it. But declining does not end the case. Expect a rejection and plan your next step.
        </div>

        <div class="disclaimer">
          <strong>Disclaimer:</strong> This article is for information only and does not constitute legal advice. For complex cases, consult a solicitor or Citizens Advice.
        </div>

        <h2>Sources</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>Private Parking Sector Single Code of Practice, version 1.1. Annex C, section 3.1.3 Note 2, and the scope note saying notes are best practice. <strong>Primary.</strong> <a href="https://irp.cdn-website.com/262226a6/files/uploaded/sector+single+Code+of+Practice+Version+1.1+130426-2.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">IPC-hosted PDF</a></li>
          <li>Protection of Freedoms Act 2012, Schedule 4, paragraph 3. <strong>Primary.</strong> <a href="https://www.legislation.gov.uk/cy/ukpga/2012/9/schedule/4/paragraph/3/1" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Legislation.gov.uk</a></li>
          <li>Times24 UK Limited, reply to appeal requesting photo ID, Gatwick North Terminal. <strong>Primary.</strong> Redacted copy on this site.</li>
          <li>Independent Appeals Service, "Before you appeal". <strong>Primary.</strong> <a href="https://theias.org/before-you-appeal" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">theias.org/before-you-appeal</a></li>
          <li>GOV.UK, "Challenging a parking ticket". POPLA for BPA members, IAS for IPC members. <strong>Secondary.</strong> <a href="https://www.gov.uk/parking-tickets/challenging-a-ticket" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">GOV.UK</a></li>
          <li>Citizens Advice, "Appealing a parking ticket". 28 days from rejection for BPA members. <strong>Secondary.</strong> <a href="https://www.citizensadvice.org.uk/law-and-courts/parking-tickets/appealing-a-parking-ticket/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Citizens Advice</a></li>
        </ol>
      </article>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .blog-post figure {
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
  }

  .blog-post figure img,
  .protected-image img {
    display: block;
    max-width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    pointer-events: none;
    user-select: none;
    -webkit-user-select: none;
  }

  .blog-post figcaption {
    margin-top: .5rem;
    color: var(--light);
    font-size: .85rem;
  }

</style>

<script>
  document.querySelectorAll('.protected-image img').forEach((image) => {
    image.addEventListener('contextmenu', (event) => event.preventDefault());
    image.addEventListener('dragstart', (event) => event.preventDefault());
  });
</script>
